w("## 7. Defects and caveats observed (state them as limitations; the shipped code was left unchanged)\n")
w("1. Evaluation clips test targets to the training range (4.2): MAE/R2/direction of the deep tiers are not computed against the real closes.")
w("2. The sentiment channel is zero for every training row and carries synthetic template headlines in the test part (4.2); `README` and the UI disclose the synthetic fallback, but the ablation cannot measure a sentiment effect.")
w("3. Multi-step forecasts roll the Close forward and overwrite Open/High/Low with it (`forecast.py`), keep volume and sentiment constant, and the band uses sqrt(h) growth from one-step residuals; the shipped 5-day forecasts fall 6-28 % for nine of ten tickers (4.1) and missed the realised moves (4.3).")
w("4. FedAvg averages models trained on differently scaled price series (per-ticker MinMax), then scores the global model with each ticker's own scaler.")
w("5. `data_loader.load_ticker` overwrites `data/<T>.csv` on every live pull; `/api/history` therefore performs a network download per request (see latency), and artefacts can be older than the CSVs (the Forecast screen shows a banner explaining this).")
w("6. The synthetic fallback seeds with Python's `hash(ticker)`, which changes between processes (PYTHONHASHSEED); the synthetic headlines are therefore not reproducible unless the hash seed is fixed (the experiments set PYTHONHASHSEED=0).")
w("7. Classical baselines ARIMA/SARIMAX/Prophet forecast the whole 101-day test span without refitting, whereas RF/SVR use actual lagged values one step ahead; the table mixes the two protocols (as in the base paper).")
w("8. No MIL, no social-media text, no precision/recall/F1 in the product (section 1).\n")

